    subtitle: "Scientists measuring the melting ice-sheets in the Arctic",
    content: `<p>The Arctic scientists, Clara and David, stood on the vast white expanse of the glacier. They were here to measure the melting ice-sheets, a critical study of global climate change. Clara, who was also a keen <button class="word-btn" data-word="lexicographer">lexicographer</button> of weather terms in her free time, watched the digital screens. The data they were collecting would <button class="word-btn" data-word="dilate">dilate</button> the understanding of climate models worldwide. They lived in a small, dome tent, a simple <button class="word-btn" data-word="conveyance">conveyance</button> that protected them from the wind. Clara knew they had to be <button class="word-btn" data-word="wary">wary</button> of the ice, which was unstable and cracked under their weight, leaving them <button class="word-btn" data-word="vulnerable">vulnerable</button> to falling.</p><p>The ice was a dynamic, shifting world. They had to <button class="word-btn" data-word="engage">engage</button> in daily measurements, checking the depth of the cracks. Clara noticed a deep crevice in the glacier, its walls glowing with a dark blue light. The glacier was a <button class="word-btn" data-word="decrepit">decrepit</button> giant, melting slowly under the sun. David, a young scientist who was a bit of a <button class="word-btn" data-word="maverick">maverick</button> in the institute, suggested they descend into the crevice to take samples. Clara had some <button class="word-btn" data-word="cognizance">cognizance</button> of the danger, but she agreed, refusing to <button class="word-btn" data-word="badger">badger</button> him with endless safety rules. They set up their ropes, preparing for a difficult descent.</p><p>They sank into the blue dark. The walls of the crevice were covered in a <button class="word-btn" data-word="glutinous">glutinous</button> layer of ice-slush, making the climb slippery. They had to <button class="word-btn" data-word="grapple">grapple</button> with the cold, their fingers stiffening in the gloves. David worked to collect samples of the ancient ice, which had been frozen for an <button class="word-btn" data-word="eon">eon</button>. The air in the crevice was wet and <button class="word-btn" data-word="humid">humid</button>, smelling of old rock. They did not hear the sudden crack of the ice above, a sound that signaled a coming collapse. Clara shouted to David to climb, their climb a race against the falling ice, their breath held with <button class="word-btn" data-word="bate">bate</button>d expectation.</p><p>They emerged from the crevice just as it collapsed, a narrow escape. They sat on the snow, their hearts beating fast, their relief <button class="word-btn" data-word="uninhibited">uninhibited</button>. Clara knew their <button class="word-btn" data-word="ordeal">ordeal</button> would help to prove their theory, providing vital data. Their research was a <button class="word-btn" data-word="partisan">partisan</button> battle for environmental awareness, facing heavy opposition from industrial lobby groups who offered <button class="word-btn" data-word="specious">specious</button> arguments about the glacier's stability. Clara offered a short <button class="word-btn" data-word="adage">adage</button> about the wisdom of their <button class="word-btn" data-word="forebears">forebears</button>, who had respected the ice. They packed their gear, ready to return to the ship. The <button class="word-btn" data-word="allure">allure</button> of the north was strong, and they hoped the ice would not <button class="word-btn" data-word="wax">wax</button> too thin before their return. A <button class="word-btn" data-word="throng">throng</button> of local guides waited at the base camp, led by a quiet <button class="word-btn" data-word="intermediary">intermediary</button>. Clara refused to offer <button class="word-btn" data-word="unpalatable">unpalatable</button> reports to the press, but she would tell the truth. They had survived, and the <button class="word-btn" data-word="volatile">volatile</button> weather would not stop them. David was a <button class="word-btn" data-word="rabid">rabid</button> supporter of environmental action, and he was ready. Clara, usually <button class="word-btn" data-word="fussy">fussy</button> about details, simply smiled, glad to be alive, and they left the <button class="word-btn" data-word="noisome">noisome</button> fumes of the snowmobile engines behind.</p>`
