    title: "The Spectral Lighthouse",
    subtitle: "A ghost guiding lost sailors to the afterlife",
    content: `<p>On the edge of the jagged cliffs of the outer rim, the Spectral Lighthouse stood silent. The lighthouse was a <button class="word-btn" data-word="commodious">commodious</button> stone structure, its light pointing toward the stellar void. Inside, the keeper was a ghost, a quiet spirit named Captain Valen. Valen had died in a shipwreck years ago, and now he had a <button class="word-btn" data-word="reverent">reverent</button> duty: to guide lost sailors to the afterlife, which they called the final <button class="word-btn" data-word="terminus">terminus</button>. Valen’s presence was a quiet, peaceful force, his mind filled with an eternal <button class="word-btn" data-word="serenity">serenity</button>.</p><p>The lighthouse was far from a <button class="word-btn" data-word="dingy">dingy</button> prison, its walls covered in ancient maps and logs. Valen would <button class="word-btn" data-word="hover">hover</button> near the light, checking the digital coordinates. He used a rare, blue <button class="word-btn" data-word="pigment">pigment</button> to record the ships in his logs, a record he kept in the lighthouse <button class="word-btn" data-word="archives">archives</button>. He was a <button class="word-btn" data-word="blithe">blithe</button> spirit, accepting his fate without complaint. He watched for the arrival of new ships, his light guiding them past the dangerous reefs.</p><p>One evening, a pirate ship approached the cliffs. The pirate captain, a loud and arrogant man who wore a green, <button class="word-btn" data-word="bilious">bilious</button> coat, was searching for a secret treasure. The pirate captain spoke with a smooth, <button class="word-btn" data-word="glib">glib</button> voice, trying to <button class="word-btn" data-word="tantalize">tantalize</button> Valen with promises of gold if he would show the path. He warned that his crew would <button class="word-btn" data-word="confine">confine</button> the ghost in an iron cage, a threat that was a form of dark <button class="word-btn" data-word="metaphysical">metaphysical</button> torture. The pirate captain tried to <button class="word-btn" data-word="reproach">reproach</button> Valen’s silence, but Valen refused to speak. He was a quiet <button class="word-btn" data-word="pacifist">pacifist</button>, refusing to help the traitors.</p><p>Valen gave the pirate ship a warning, a sudden, sharp <button class="word-btn" data-word="flick">flick</button> of the light. The warning was a clear, <button class="word-btn" data-word="discernible">discernible</button> sign of danger, but the <button class="word-btn" data-word="gullible">gullible</button> pirates ignored it, their ship hitting the reef. The impact did <button class="word-btn" data-word="maim">maim</button> the vessel, which began to sink. The ghost felt no <button class="word-btn" data-word="incredulous">incredulous</button> surprise, nor did he want to <button class="word-btn" data-word="avenge">avenge</button> his own death. He watched the crew escape, their spirits rising from the water like a rising <button class="word-btn" data-word="phoenix">phoenix</button>. He guided them to safety, their former <button class="word-btn" data-word="malaise">malaise</button> replaced by peace. Valen sat in his tower, eating a simple, <button class="word-btn" data-word="savory">savory</button> bowl of soup, his mind free from any <button class="word-btn" data-word="homely">homely</button> details of life. He knew his work was in his own <button class="word-btn" data-word="inimitable">inimitable</button> style, a legacy that would escape any <button class="word-btn" data-word="forensic">forensic</button> search of the living, and he would not allow the court to write a sad <button class="word-btn" data-word="eulogy">eulogy</button> for him, his light a sign of hope, a success that would <button class="word-btn" data-word="evince">evince</button> the power of peace over war.</p>`
